-- Adds the two Module 2 lessons scoped earlier today: a second algorithm
-- (decision trees) and ensemble methods -- the two gaps that needed a new
-- lesson rather than an addition to an existing one, since every other
-- lesson in this module teaches gradient-descent linear regression and
-- these are a genuinely different mechanism with no natural host lesson.
-- Deliberately paired and placed together, in this order, at the end of
-- Module 2: decision trees first (a real, different algorithm class, no
-- calculus required), then ensemble methods (what happens when you combine
-- many of them) -- the second lesson doesn't make sense without the first.
--
-- Anchored to the stable slug 'how-do-we-know-a-model-is-good' (Module 2's
-- current last lesson), safe regardless of what else has already run. Same
-- negate-then-restore trick as every other lesson insertion this session;
-- lesson_progress/lesson_quiz_questions key off lesson_id, never
-- order_index. Both code examples verified against a real Node.js
-- simulation before writing this migration.

UPDATE public.lessons SET order_index = -order_index
WHERE order_index > (SELECT order_index FROM public.lessons WHERE slug = 'how-do-we-know-a-model-is-good');
UPDATE public.lessons SET order_index = -order_index + 2 WHERE order_index < 0;

WITH new_lesson AS (
  INSERT INTO public.lessons (module_number, order_index, title, slug, summary, coin_cost, is_published)
  VALUES (
    2,
    (SELECT order_index FROM public.lessons WHERE slug = 'how-do-we-know-a-model-is-good') + 1,
    'Decision Trees: Learning by Asking Questions',
    'decision-trees',
    'A real second algorithm -- no weights, no gradient descent, no calculus. Just the right yes/no questions in the right order.',
    10,
    true
  )
  RETURNING id
), ins_content AS (
  INSERT INTO public.lesson_content (lesson_id, content)
  SELECT id, '{"hook":"A decision tree doesn''t do any of the math from the last few lessons -- no weights, no gradient descent, no cost function. It just asks the right yes/no questions in the right order.","explanation":"A decision tree makes a prediction by asking a sequence of yes/no questions about the data, branching a different way depending on the answer, until it reaches a leaf that holds a final prediction. Unlike linear regression, there is no gradient descent, no weights, no calculus at all -- training a tree just means choosing, at each branch, the single best question to ask next. \"Best\" is measured by impurity: how mixed the labels are in a group. A pure group (everyone in it has the same label) has zero impurity; a 50/50 mixed group has maximum impurity. At every branch, the tree tries out different possible questions and picks whichever one splits the data into the PUREST resulting groups -- the one that removes the most confusion in one step. It repeats this recursively on each resulting group until every leaf is pure enough (or a stopping rule kicks in), building up the whole tree one question at a time.","analogy":"It is exactly how a good game of 20 Questions works -- a skilled player does not ask random questions, they ask the ONE question that splits the remaining possibilities as evenly and informatively as possible. \"Does it have fur?\" (splits animals roughly in half) beats \"Is it a golden retriever?\" (splits off almost nothing). A decision tree picks its questions the exact same way, just measured with a formula instead of intuition.","fun_fact":"Decision trees are the actual building block behind Random Forest and XGBoost -- two of the most-used, most-winning algorithms in real-world data science competitions. Understanding one tree is most of the way to understanding why those far more powerful methods work.","try_it":"Think of a real yes/no question that would split a group of your classmates into two PURER groups by some trait (e.g. \"do they have a phone with a cracked screen\"). What question would split them most evenly and informatively?","code":"def gini(labels):\n    total = len(labels)\n    if total == 0:\n        return 0\n    counts = {}\n    for l in labels:\n        counts[l] = counts.get(l, 0) + 1\n    impurity = 1\n    for count in counts.values():\n        p = count / total\n        impurity -= p ** 2\n    return impurity\n\nemails = [\n    {\"has_link\": True,  \"exclamations\": 3, \"label\": \"spam\"},\n    {\"has_link\": True,  \"exclamations\": 0, \"label\": \"spam\"},\n    {\"has_link\": False, \"exclamations\": 5, \"label\": \"not spam\"},\n    {\"has_link\": False, \"exclamations\": 0, \"label\": \"not spam\"},\n    {\"has_link\": True,  \"exclamations\": 1, \"label\": \"spam\"},\n    {\"has_link\": False, \"exclamations\": 1, \"label\": \"not spam\"},\n]\n\nall_labels = [e[\"label\"] for e in emails]\nprint(f\"Gini impurity of the whole dataset: {gini(all_labels):.2f}\")\n\n# Candidate split 1: has_link\nwith_link = [e[\"label\"] for e in emails if e[\"has_link\"]]\nwithout_link = [e[\"label\"] for e in emails if not e[\"has_link\"]]\nweighted_gini_link = (len(with_link) / len(emails)) * gini(with_link) + (len(without_link) / len(emails)) * gini(without_link)\nprint(f\"Weighted Gini after splitting on has_link: {weighted_gini_link:.2f}\")\n\n# Candidate split 2: exclamations >= 1\nhigh_excl = [e[\"label\"] for e in emails if e[\"exclamations\"] >= 1]\nlow_excl = [e[\"label\"] for e in emails if e[\"exclamations\"] < 1]\nweighted_gini_excl = (len(high_excl) / len(emails)) * gini(high_excl) + (len(low_excl) / len(emails)) * gini(low_excl)\nprint(f\"Weighted Gini after splitting on exclamations >= 1: {weighted_gini_excl:.2f}\")\n# has_link produces PERFECTLY pure groups (0.00) -- the tree picks it first.\n# exclamations >= 1 does not improve on the unsplit data at all (0.50 -> 0.50).","visual":{"caption":"How a tree picks which question to ask","steps":[{"emoji":"🌳","label":"Whole Dataset","caption":"Every example, mixed together, at the root."},{"emoji":"❓","label":"Try Candidate Questions","caption":"For each possible split, measure how pure the resulting groups would be."},{"emoji":"✂️","label":"Pick the Best Split","caption":"Choose whichever question removes the most impurity."},{"emoji":"🔁","label":"Repeat on Each Branch","caption":"Split each resulting group again, the same way."},{"emoji":"🍃","label":"Pure Leaves","caption":"Stop once a group is pure enough -- that becomes the final prediction."}]},"practice":{"prompt":"Two candidate splits are tried on the same dataset. Split A results in weighted Gini impurity 0.0. Split B results in weighted Gini impurity 0.5 (no improvement over the unsplit data). Which split should the tree actually choose?","options":["Split A -- the lower weighted impurity means it separates the classes more cleanly","Split B, since 0.5 is a larger number","Either -- the impurity score does not matter","Neither -- trees never use impurity to choose splits"],"correct_index":0,"feedback":"A lower weighted Gini impurity means the resulting groups are purer (more class-separated) -- that is exactly the signal a decision tree uses to pick its splits, and it always favors greater purity."},"code_practice":{"instructions":"Compute the weighted Gini impurity for a THIRD candidate split: exclamations >= 3. Follow the exact same pattern as the has_link and exclamations >= 1 splits above.","starter":"def gini(labels):\n    total = len(labels)\n    if total == 0:\n        return 0\n    counts = {}\n    for l in labels:\n        counts[l] = counts.get(l, 0) + 1\n    impurity = 1\n    for count in counts.values():\n        p = count / total\n        impurity -= p ** 2\n    return impurity\n\nemails = [\n    {\"has_link\": True,  \"exclamations\": 3, \"label\": \"spam\"},\n    {\"has_link\": True,  \"exclamations\": 0, \"label\": \"spam\"},\n    {\"has_link\": False, \"exclamations\": 5, \"label\": \"not spam\"},\n    {\"has_link\": False, \"exclamations\": 0, \"label\": \"not spam\"},\n    {\"has_link\": True,  \"exclamations\": 1, \"label\": \"spam\"},\n    {\"has_link\": False, \"exclamations\": 1, \"label\": \"not spam\"},\n]\n\n# TODO: compute weighted_gini_excl3 for the split \"exclamations >= 3\"\n# (hint: filter into a high group and a low group, same as the examples\n# from the lesson, then combine their gini scores weighted by group size)\n\nprint(f\"Weighted Gini after splitting on exclamations >= 3: {weighted_gini_excl3:.2f}\")","check_pattern":"exclamations.*>=\\s*3","success_message":"That comes out to 0.50 too -- no better than the unsplit data. has_link (0.00) is still clearly the best first question this tree could ask.","hint":"high = [e[\"label\"] for e in emails if e[\"exclamations\"] >= 3], low = [e[\"label\"] for e in emails if e[\"exclamations\"] < 3], then weighted_gini_excl3 = (len(high)/len(emails))*gini(high) + (len(low)/len(emails))*gini(low)"}}'::jsonb FROM new_lesson
  RETURNING lesson_id
)
INSERT INTO public.lesson_quiz_questions (lesson_id, order_index, question, options, correct_index, explanation)
SELECT ins_content.lesson_id, q.order_index, q.question, q.options::jsonb, q.correct_index, q.explanation
FROM ins_content, (VALUES
  (1, 'What does a decision tree use to decide WHICH question to ask at each split?', '["An impurity measure like Gini, choosing whichever split produces the purest resulting groups","Gradient descent, the same as linear regression","A random coin flip at every branch","The alphabetical order of the feature names"]', 0, 'The tree tries candidate splits and picks whichever one most reduces impurity -- that is the entire "training" process.'),
  (2, 'What does a Gini impurity of 0 mean for a group of examples?', '["The group is completely pure -- every example in it has the same label","The group is maximally mixed between classes","The group is empty","Gini impurity of 0 is not a real possible value"]', 0, '0 is the minimum possible impurity -- it means there is no mixing of classes left in that group at all.'),
  (3, 'In the code example, why does the tree prefer splitting on has_link over exclamations >= 1?', '["has_link produces a weighted Gini of 0.00 (perfectly pure groups), while exclamations >= 1 stays at 0.50 (no improvement at all)","exclamations >= 1 is alphabetically later","has_link uses fewer characters to write in code","Both splits are equally good, so the tree picks randomly"]', 0, 'has_link fully separates spam from not-spam (impurity drops to 0); exclamations >= 1 does not improve on the unsplit data whatsoever.'),
  (4, 'Does training a decision tree require gradient descent or calculus?', '["No -- it just repeatedly picks whichever available question best reduces impurity","Yes, exactly the same gradient descent loop as linear regression","Yes, but only for classification trees, not regression trees","Decision trees cannot be trained at all"]', 0, 'This is the core contrast with every other algorithm taught so far in this module -- no weights, no cost function, no calculus.'),
  (5, 'What happens after a decision tree picks its first split?', '["It repeats the exact same process (try candidate questions, pick the purest split) on each resulting group","Training stops immediately after one split, always","It picks the next split completely at random","It goes back and re-evaluates whether has_link was really the best root split, forever"]', 0, 'The tree recurses -- each resulting group gets split again the same way, building the tree one level at a time.'),
  (6, 'What real-world, high-performing algorithms are decision trees the building block of?', '["Random Forest and XGBoost","Gradient descent and backpropagation","ROC curves and AUC","Tokenization and embeddings"]', 0, 'Both are ensembles built from many decision trees -- understanding one tree is most of the way to understanding why they work.'),
  (7, 'The "20 Questions" analogy in this lesson illustrates what?', '["A good question is one that splits the remaining possibilities most evenly and informatively -- the same principle a tree uses via impurity","20 Questions has nothing to do with how decision trees work","A decision tree can only ever ask exactly 20 questions","Impurity and question quality are unrelated concepts"]', 0, 'A skilled 20-Questions player picks broad, evenly-splitting questions first -- exactly what choosing the lowest-impurity split does mathematically.')
) AS q(order_index, question, options, correct_index, explanation);

WITH new_lesson AS (
  INSERT INTO public.lessons (module_number, order_index, title, slug, summary, coin_cost, is_published)
  VALUES (
    2,
    (SELECT order_index FROM public.lessons WHERE slug = 'how-do-we-know-a-model-is-good') + 2,
    'Ensemble Methods: Why a Committee Beats One Expert',
    'ensemble-methods',
    'One decision tree can be wrong. A hundred slightly-different trees voting together are very often right.',
    10,
    true
  )
  RETURNING id
), ins_content AS (
  INSERT INTO public.lesson_content (lesson_id, content)
  SELECT id, '{"hook":"One decision tree can be wrong. Ask 100 slightly-different trees the same question and take a vote, and the group is very often right -- even when several of them, alone, were not.","explanation":"An ensemble method combines predictions from MULTIPLE models instead of trusting just one. The most common approach, bagging (bootstrap aggregating), trains many versions of the same kind of model -- often decision trees -- each on a different random subset of the training data, so each one ends up slightly different and makes slightly different mistakes. To make a final prediction, every model in the ensemble votes (for classification) or their predictions get averaged (for regression), and the majority or average becomes the answer. This works because each individual tree''s errors tend to be somewhat random and independent -- one tree might get confused by an unusual example, but it is far less likely that MOST of the trees get confused by the exact same example in the exact same way. Random Forest is exactly this idea applied specifically to decision trees, and it is one of the most reliable, widely-used algorithms in real-world machine learning for precisely this reason.","analogy":"It is a second opinion, times 100. Any single doctor can misdiagnose a tricky case -- but if 100 doctors independently examine the same patient and 90 of them agree, that agreement is far more trustworthy than any one opinion alone, even though every individual doctor is still fallible.","fun_fact":"Random Forest and XGBoost (a more advanced ensemble technique called boosting) are consistently among the top-performing algorithms in real data science competitions on structured, spreadsheet-like data -- often beating far more complex deep learning models on exactly this kind of data.","try_it":"Think of a real decision you would trust more from a group vote than from one person alone (a panel of judges, a jury, a group of doctors). Why does combining several independent opinions tend to produce a better result than trusting just one?","code":"# Three slightly different trees, each imperfect in its own way -- as if\n# each were trained on a different random subset of the email data\ndef tree_1(email):\n    return \"spam\" if email[\"has_link\"] else \"not spam\"\n\ndef tree_2(email):\n    return \"spam\" if email[\"exclamations\"] >= 2 else \"not spam\"\n\ndef tree_3(email):\n    return \"spam\" if email[\"has_link\"] or email[\"exclamations\"] >= 3 else \"not spam\"\n\ndef ensemble_predict(email, trees):\n    votes = [tree(email) for tree in trees]\n    return max(set(votes), key=votes.count)\n\ntrees = [tree_1, tree_2, tree_3]\n\n# A genuinely spammy email (lots of exclamations) that happens to have NO link\ntest_email = {\"has_link\": False, \"exclamations\": 3}\n\nfor tree in trees:\n    print(f\"{tree.__name__}: {tree(test_email)}\")\n\nfinal = ensemble_predict(test_email, trees)\nprint(f\"Ensemble (majority vote): {final}\")\n# tree_1 alone gets this WRONG (\"not spam\", since there is no link) --\n# but tree_2 and tree_3 both correctly say \"spam\", so the majority vote\n# overrides tree_1''s mistake.","visual":{"caption":"A committee of imperfect trees, voting together","steps":[{"emoji":"🌲","label":"Train Tree 1","caption":"On one random subset of the data."},{"emoji":"🌳","label":"Train Tree 2","caption":"On a DIFFERENT random subset -- slightly different from Tree 1."},{"emoji":"🌴","label":"Train Tree 3 (and more)","caption":"Each tree ends up a little different, with different mistakes."},{"emoji":"🗳️","label":"Every Tree Votes","caption":"Each tree predicts independently on the new example."},{"emoji":"🏆","label":"Majority Wins","caption":"The most common prediction becomes the ensemble''s final answer."}]},"practice":{"prompt":"In the code example, tree_1 alone predicts \"not spam\" for the test email -- the wrong answer. Why does the ensemble still get it right?","options":["The majority of the three trees (2 out of 3) voted \"spam\", outvoting tree_1''s mistake","The ensemble always overrides tree_1 specifically, regardless of its answer","It is random luck with no real explanation","Ensembles cannot actually correct any individual tree''s mistake"],"correct_index":0,"feedback":"tree_2 and tree_3 both predicted \"spam\", so the majority vote (2 out of 3) correctly overrides tree_1''s lone incorrect prediction -- exactly why ensembles tend to be more robust than any single model."},"code_practice":{"instructions":"The ensemble_predict function is missing its majority-vote logic. Complete the TODO so it returns whichever prediction appears most often among the votes.","starter":"def tree_1(email):\n    return \"spam\" if email[\"has_link\"] else \"not spam\"\n\ndef tree_2(email):\n    return \"spam\" if email[\"exclamations\"] >= 2 else \"not spam\"\n\ndef tree_3(email):\n    return \"spam\" if email[\"has_link\"] or email[\"exclamations\"] >= 3 else \"not spam\"\n\ndef ensemble_predict(email, trees):\n    votes = [tree(email) for tree in trees]\n    # TODO: return the most common prediction among votes (the majority vote)\n    return None\n\ntest_email = {\"has_link\": False, \"exclamations\": 3}\nprint(ensemble_predict(test_email, [tree_1, tree_2, tree_3]))","check_pattern":"max\\(.*votes\\.count\\)|Counter\\(votes\\)","success_message":"That is a real majority vote -- the ensemble now correctly outputs \"spam\", overriding tree_1''s lone wrong guess.","hint":"Try: return max(set(votes), key=votes.count)"}}'::jsonb FROM new_lesson
  RETURNING lesson_id
)
INSERT INTO public.lesson_quiz_questions (lesson_id, order_index, question, options, correct_index, explanation)
SELECT ins_content.lesson_id, q.order_index, q.question, q.options::jsonb, q.correct_index, q.explanation
FROM ins_content, (VALUES
  (1, 'What is an ensemble method?', '["Combining predictions from multiple models instead of relying on just one","A single, extra-large decision tree with more branches than usual","A technique that only works for regression, never classification","Another name for gradient descent"]', 0, 'An ensemble is a collection of models whose individual predictions get combined -- voting for classification, averaging for regression.'),
  (2, 'In the code example, what does tree_1 alone predict for the test email, and is it correct?', '["\"not spam\" -- which is wrong; the ensemble (majority vote) correctly says \"spam\"","\"spam\" -- which is correct on its own","tree_1 refuses to make a prediction","tree_1 and the ensemble always agree by definition"]', 0, 'tree_1 only looks at has_link, which is False here, so it misses the spam signal the other two trees catch.'),
  (3, 'How does the ensemble in the code example decide its final prediction?', '["Majority vote across all three trees'' individual predictions","It always trusts whichever tree was defined first in the code","It averages the trees'' code together into one new tree","It picks randomly among the three trees'' answers"]', 0, 'ensemble_predict counts each tree''s vote and returns whichever prediction appears most often.'),
  (4, 'What is "bagging" (bootstrap aggregating)?', '["Training multiple models on different random subsets of the data, then combining their predictions","Deleting the training data after each model is trained","A technique that only applies to neural networks","Running the exact same model twice and averaging identical results"]', 0, 'Bagging is specifically about training on different random subsets so each model ends up meaningfully different from the others.'),
  (5, 'Why can an ensemble of imperfect models often outperform any single one of them?', '["Individual models'' mistakes tend to be somewhat independent, so a majority vote cancels out some of those errors","Ensembles are magic and have no real explanation","Every individual model in an ensemble is always perfectly accurate on its own","Averaging predictions always increases every individual error"]', 0, 'One tree might get confused by an unusual example, but it is unlikely that MOST trees get confused by that same example in the same way.'),
  (6, 'What is Random Forest?', '["An ensemble of many decision trees, each trained on a different random subset of data, combined by majority vote or averaging","A single very deep decision tree","A synonym for gradient descent","A technique for visualizing datasets, not a prediction method"]', 0, 'Random Forest is bagging applied specifically to decision trees -- the exact idea this lesson walks through in code.'),
  (7, 'What is the main tradeoff of using an ensemble instead of one model?', '["More computation (training and running many models instead of one) in exchange for typically better, more robust accuracy","There is no tradeoff -- ensembles are strictly better with no downside","Ensembles are always less accurate than a single model","Ensembles eliminate the need for any training data at all"]', 0, 'Running many models costs more compute than running one -- a real cost paid in exchange for the reliability gain.')
) AS q(order_index, question, options, correct_index, explanation);

NOTIFY pgrst, 'reload schema';
